# Line endings, made explicit.
#
# The repo had `core.autocrlf = true` and NO .gitattributes, so every blob was LF while
# the working tree was CRLF (368 tracked text files) and 82 files were LF — the state was
# not even uniform. Measured consequences, 2026-09-16:
#   - `edit` cannot match multi-line content in a CRLF file, so multi-line edits had to go
#     through bespoke scripts that use the file's own EOL
#   - Biome flags every line ending, roughly DOUBLING the error density of a CRLF file
#     (0.061-0.077 errors/line) against an LF one (0.032-0.045)
#   - comparing a checked-out file with `git show` flags every text file as different
#
# `eol=lf` overrides `core.autocrlf` for every file matched here, so the working tree and
# the blobs agree from now on. A refresh of the working tree is still needed once:
#   git checkout-index --force --all
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-only shells genuinely need CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Not text. `text=auto` would usually detect these, but a mis-detection here corrupts a
# binary silently, and the failure surfaces much later.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.avif binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.svg text eol=lf
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.eot binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.xlsx binary
*.xls binary
*.docx binary
*.mp4 binary
*.mp3 binary
